t per column.\n\nThus each non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1) can cover at most one point per column, while vertical lines can cover all points in a column.\n\nThus to cover columns with many points, we need vertical lines for columns where the number of points > number of non-vertical non-sunny lines that can cover them.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines. They could be vertical or horizontal or slope -1. We need to cover all columns.\n\nWe have n columns (x=1,...,n). The column sizes are h_c = n+1-c.\n\nIf we have n-2 non-sunny lines, we can have at most n-2 vertical lines (if we choose all vertical). But we need to cover n columns. If we have n-2 vertical lines, we would need to cover the remaining 2 columns using horizontal or slope -1 lines. But those lines can only cover one point per column, not all points. So we need to ensure those columns have at most 1 point each (i.e., columns of size 1). The only column of size 1 is column n (h_n=1). Column n-1 has size 2. So we cannot cover column n-1 with a non-vertical line only, because it has 2 points. However, we could use sunny lines to cover some points in column n-1. Since we have 2 sunny lines, they can each cover at most one point in column n-1. So we could cover both points of column n-1 using the 2 sunny lines, leaving no need for a vertical line for column n-1. Then we could have vertical lines for all other columns except maybe column n-2? Let's see.\n\nColumn n-2 has size 3. Sunny lines can cover at most 2 points there (since we have only 2 sunny lines). So we need at least one non-sunny line to cover the remaining point(s). Could be a vertical line x=n-2, covering all 3 points, but then we would have vertical lines for all columns except maybe n-1 and n? Actually we need vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (size >=4). That's n-3 columns. So total vertical lines needed: columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines) plus maybe column n-2 (if we don't use vertical line there). But we have n-2 non-sunny lines total. So we could allocate n-3 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3, and use the remaining non-sunny line for column n-2 (vertical) or horizontal/slope -1 covering some points. Let's see if we can allocate.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines. Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 lines). Then we have (n-2) - (n-3) = 1 non-sunny line left. That line could be vertical x=n-2, covering all points in column n-2. Then we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (n-2 vertical lines). Then we have sunny lines covering column n-1 (2 points) and column n (1 point). That would be a valid configuration: n-2 vertical lines (non-sunny) + 2 sunny lines covering the remaining points. But we need total lines = n. However, we have n-2 vertical lines + 2 sunny lines = n lines. So that seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to check if the sunny lines can be chosen to cover the points in column n-1 and column n while not being vertical/horizontal/slope -1. Let's try to construct such lines.\n\nWe need to cover points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) (the two points in column n-1) and point (n,1) (the only point in column n). We have two sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point to a sunny line, possibly covering multiple points across columns.\n\nOne sunny line could pass through (n-1,1) and (n,1). But that line would be horizontal (y=1), which is non-sunny. So not allowed. Another sunny line could pass through (n-1,2) and (n,1). The slope would be (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, which is slope -1, also non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both a point in column n-1 and the point in column n, because any line connecting (n,1) to any point (n-1,b) will have slope (b-1)/(-1) = 1-b, which is integer. For b=1, slope 0 (horizontal). For b=2, slope -1. For b=3, slope -2 (which is sunny). But column n-1 only has b=1,2. So we cannot connect (n,1) to any point in column n-1 with a sunny slope. So we need to cover (n,1) with a sunny line that does not pass through any point in column n-1. That means the sunny line must pass through (n,1) and some other point in a different column (maybe column n-2 or earlier). But then we need to also cover the two points in column n-1 with the other sunny line(s). Since we have only 2 sunny lines, we could assign one sunny line to cover (n,1) and some other point (maybe in column n-2), and the other sunny line to cover both points in column n-1? But a line cannot pass through both (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) because they have same x-coordinate, so that would be vertical, non-sunny. So we cannot cover both points in column n-1 with a single line. So we need two separate lines for those two points. That would require at least 3 sunny lines: one for (n,1) and one each for (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). However, we could possibly have a sunny line covering (n-1,1) and some other point in column n-2, and another sunny line covering (n-1,2) and some other point in column n-2, while (n,1) is covered by a vertical line? But vertical line x=n is non-sunny, but we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, which we used for columns 1,...,n-2. Actually we could use vertical line x=n to cover (n,1) as a non-sunny line, but we need to have exactly k=2 sunny lines, so we can have vertical line x=n as a non-sunny line. But we need to cover column n-2 (size 3) with vertical line x=n-2 (non-sunny) and maybe also horizontal or slope -1 lines covering some points. Let's think.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines. We could allocate vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines). Then we have 1 non-sunny line left. That line could be vertical x=n-2 or x=n-1 or x=n. But we need to cover column n-1 (size 2) and column n (size 1). If we use vertical line x=n-1, that covers both points in column n-1, leaving column n to be covered by a sunny line. But we have 2 sunny lines, so we could use one sunny line to cover (n,1) and maybe some other point. The other sunny line could be used to cover some other points not covered by vertical lines? But all other columns are already covered by vertical lines. So the second sunny line would be redundant. But we need exactly 2 sunny lines, not at most. So we need to use both sunny lines, but they could be used to cover points already covered by vertical lines (overlap allowed). Overlap is allowed because lines can intersect and cover same points multiple times. The problem only requires each point to be on at least one line; it doesn't forbid multiple coverage. So we could have vertical lines covering all columns, and then add two sunny lines that cover some points (maybe overlapping). That would be a valid configuration: n-2 vertical lines (cover all points) + 2 sunny lines (any sunny lines). But we need exactly n distinct lines. If we have n-2 vertical lines (cover columns 1,...,n-2) and also vertical lines for columns n-1 and n? Actually we need n lines total. If we have n-2 vertical lines, we need 2 more lines (sunny). But we need to cover all columns. The vertical lines we have are n-2 of them; we need to decide which columns they cover. We could choose vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 lines). Then we have columns n-1 and n not covered by vertical lines. But we have 2 sunny lines to cover those columns. However, as we saw, we cannot cover column n-1 (both points) with a single sunny line, because that would be vertical. So we need at least 2 sunny lines to cover the two points in column n-1. But we also need to cover column n (point (n,1)). That would require a third line (sunny) or a vertical line x=n (non-sunny). But we have only 2 sunny lines. So we cannot cover column n-1 and column n with only 2 sunny lines if we don't have vertical lines for those columns. So we need to allocate vertical lines for at least one of those columns. Let's try vertical line x=n-1 (non-sunny) covering both points in column n-1. Then we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (n-2 lines) plus vertical line x=n-1 (makes n-1 vertical lines). That's too many non-sunny lines (n-1). But we only have n-2 non-sunny lines (since total lines n, sunny k=2). So we cannot have vertical lines for both columns n-2 and n-1 and also all earlier columns. Let's try a different allocation: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines). Then we have 2 non-sunny lines left (since n-2 non-sunny total). Use one vertical line for column n-2 (covers its 3 points). Use one vertical line for column n-1 (covers its 2 points). Then column n (size 1) is uncovered. Use a sunny line to cover (n,1). That's one sunny line used. The second sunny line can be any sunny line (maybe covering some other point already covered). So we have n-3 vertical lines (columns 1,...,n-3) + vertical line x=n-2 + vertical line x=n-1 = n-1 vertical lines. That's n-1 non-sunny lines, but we need n-2 non-sunny lines. So we have one too many non-sunny lines. So we need to reduce non-sunny lines by one, perhaps by using a non-vertical non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1) to cover some column partially, reducing need for a vertical line. Let's try to allocate non-sunny lines as follows: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines). Use a horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) to cover points (c,1) for all columns c where 1 ≤ c ≤ n (since y=1 is within the triangle for all columns). That covers (n,1) and also (n-1,1) and (n-2,1) and all earlier columns' bottom points. Then we need to cover the remaining points: column n-1 has point (n-1,2) left; column n-2 has points (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) left; column n-3 has points (n-3,2) up to (n-3,4) left, etc. But we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 covering all points in those columns, including the points (c,1) which are already covered by y=1. That's okay. So we have n-3 vertical lines + horizontal line y=1 = n-2 non-sunny lines. Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover the remaining points: (n-1,2), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). That's three points. Two sunny lines can each cover at most one point per column, so they can cover at most two points in column n-2 (since they can each cover one point there) and one point in column n-1. So we could assign sunny line 1 to cover (n-1,2) and maybe also (n-2,2) (but that would be two points in different columns, okay). Sunny line 2 could cover (n-2,3). But we need to ensure the lines are sunny (not horizontal, vertical, slope -1). Let's try to find such lines.\n\nWe need a line through (n-1,2) and (n-2,2). That's horizontal y=2, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot cover both (n-1,2) and (n-2,2) with a sunny line. So we need separate lines for each. But we have only 2 sunny lines, and we have three points to cover. So we need to cover two of them with one sunny line, but they must be in different columns and not share same y coordinate (to avoid horizontal) and not satisfy x+y constant (to avoid slope -1). Let's try to find a line through (n-1,2) and (n-2,3). Slope = (3-2)/(n-2-(n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is slope -1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Try line through (n-1,2) and (n-2,2) is horizontal, not allowed. Try line through (n-1,2) and (n-2,1) but (n-2,1) is already covered by y=1, but we could still use it. That line slope = (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, which is sunny (since slope 1 is not 0, ∞, -1). So line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) is sunny. However, (n-2,1) is already covered by y=1, but that's okay. So we can use sunny line L1: passes through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). That covers (n-1,2). It also passes through (n-2,1) which is already covered. So L1 covers (n-1,2). Then we need to cover (n-2,2) and (n-2,3). We have one sunny line left. It can cover at most one point in column n-2 (since a line cannot intersect column n-2 at two points unless vertical). So we can cover either (n-2,2) or (n-2,3). The other point would need to be covered by a non-sunny line. But we have used all non-sunny lines: n-3 vertical lines + y=1 = n-2 non-sunny lines. So we cannot add another non-sunny line. So we need to cover both (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) with the remaining sunny line, which is impossible because they share same x-coordinate. So this configuration fails.\n\nThus the attempt fails.\n\nWe need to see if any configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines exists. The solution claims impossible. Let's try to prove impossibility more generally.\n\nThe solution's argument: For k ≤ 2, column x=n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; thus one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line x=n-2. Then they consider column x=n-1 (size 2). If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. The remaining column x=n contains a single point (n,1). A line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or slope -1; both are non-sunny. Hence (n,1) would require a third sunny line, contradicting k ≤ 2. Thus k=2 impossible.\n\nWe need to examine this reasoning: They assume that for k ≤ 2, we must have a vertical line x=n-2. Let's verify that.\n\nGiven k ≤ 2, we have at most 2 sunny lines. The column x=n-2 has 3 points. Since each sunny line can intersect column n-2 at most once (if not vertical), the maximum number of points in column n-2 that can be covered by sunny lines is at most 2. So at least one point in column n-2 must be covered by a non-sunny line. However, a non-sunny line could be horizontal or slope -1, which would intersect column n-2 at at most one point (since they intersect each column at most once). So to cover all three points in column n-2, we need at least one vertical line covering the entire column. But we could also have a non-sunny line that is vertical x=n-2, covering all three points. So indeed we need a vertical line x=n-2 among the non-sunny lines. However, could we have a non-sunny line that is horizontal covering two points in column n-2? No, because horizontal line y = constant intersects column n-2 at exactly one point (since each column has distinct y values). Similarly, slope -1 line x+y = constant intersects column n-2 at exactly one point. So to cover all three points, we need a vertical line x=n-2. So that part is correct.\n\nNow, after we have vertical line x=n-2, we have used one non-sunny line. The remaining non-sunny lines (n - k - 1) can be used for other columns. For k ≤ 2, we have at most 2 sunny lines, so we have at least n-2 non-sunny lines. Actually total lines = n, so non-sunny lines = n - k ≥ n - 2. So we have at least n-2 non-sunny lines. Among them, we have used one vertical line x=n-2. So we have at least n-3 other non-sunny lines. We can allocate vertical lines for other columns as needed.\n\nNow consider column x=n-1 (size 2). If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, then both points must be covered by sunny lines (since we have at most 2 sunny lines). But we have at most 2 sunny lines total, and we already might need them for other columns. But the argument says: If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines; each sunny line can cover at most one point in column n-1, so we need exactly 2 sunny lines to cover both points. That would use up all sunny lines (since k ≤ 2). Then column n (size 1) must be covered by a non-sunny line (vertical x=n) or a sunny line. But we have no sunny lines left. So we need a non-sunny line covering (n,1). Could be vertical x=n, which is non-sunny. But we have many non-sunny lines left, so we could use vertical x=n. However, the solution says: \"A line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or slope -1; both are non-sunny. Hence (n,1) would require a third sunny line, contradicting the assumption k ≤ 2.\" Wait, why does (n,1) need a sunny line? It could be covered by a vertical line x=n, which is non-sunny. But perhaps they assume we cannot use vertical line x=n because we have already used all non-sunny lines? Let's examine.\n\nWe have n total lines. We have k sunny lines (≤2). So we have at least n-2 non-sunny lines. We have used one vertical line x=n-2. We could also use vertical line x=n-1 (if we want) and vertical line x=n (if we want). That would be three vertical lines, which is okay because we have at least n-2 non-sunny lines, and n≥3, so n-2≥1. For n≥3, n-2≥1. For n=3, n-2=1, so we have only one non-sunny line. But we need to cover column n-2 (size 3) with a vertical line x=n-2, which uses that one non-sunny line. Then we have no other non-sunny lines left. So for n=3, we cannot have vertical lines for columns n-1 and n. So we need to cover those columns with sunny lines. Let's test n=3 case.\n\nn=3: T_3 = points (a,b) with a+b ≤ 4. So points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3); (2,1),(2,2); (3,1). So columns: x=1 has 3 points, x=2 has 2 points, x=3 has 1 point. We need 3 lines total. k=2 means 2 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line. The non-sunny line must be vertical x=1 (to cover 3 points). Then we have two sunny lines to cover the remaining points: (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Each sunny line can intersect column 2 at most once, and column 3 at most once. So we need to cover (2,1) and (2,2) with two sunny lines, each covering one of them. Then we need to cover (3,1) with a sunny line, but we have no sunny lines left. So impossible. So for n=3, k=2 impossible.\n\nFor n>3, we have more non-sunny lines. Let's test n=4: T_4 points: columns: x=1:4 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); x=2:3 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); x=3:2 points (3,1),(3,2); x=4:1 point (4,1). Need 4 lines total. k=2 => 2 sunny, 2 non-sunny. Non-sunny lines must include vertical x=2 (since column x=2 has 3 points). That's one non-sunny line. The other non-sunny line could be vertical x=1, covering column 1 (4 points). Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover columns 3 and 4. Column 3 has 2 points; each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 3, so we need both sunny lines to cover those two points. Column 4 has 1 point (4,1). Could be covered by one of t