ne that passes through (n,1) and maybe also other points already covered, but that's okay. So we can have a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and any other point (maybe (1,n) but that point is not in region because a+b ≤ n+1 => (1,n) has sum n+1, so it's allowed). Actually (1,n) is allowed: a=1,b=n => sum = n+1, so it's allowed. So we could have a sunny line passing through (n,1) and (1,n). That line has slope (n-1)/(1-n) = -(n-1)/(n-1) = -1. Wait slope = (n-1)/(1-n) = -(n-1)/(n-1) = -1. So that line is slope -1, which is non-sunny (parallel to x+y=0). So we cannot use that as sunny. So we need a sunny line that is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. So we need a line that passes through (n,1) and some other point not collinear with slope -1. For example, (n,1) and (1,1) gives slope 0 (horizontal) which is non-sunny. (n,1) and (n-1,2) gives slope (2-1)/((n-1)-n) = 1/(-1) = -1 again. So many points in the region are collinear with slope -1 lines. Let's examine the set of points: For any a from 1 to n, b = n+1-a is the \"top\" point in column a. The line x+y=n+1 is a line of slope -1 passing through all points (a, n+1-a). So all points on the \"hypotenuse\" of the triangle are on a slope -1 line. So any line connecting (n,1) to any point (a,b) with a+b = n+1 will have slope -1. So to avoid slope -1, we need to connect (n,1) to a point not on that line, i.e., a+b < n+1. For example, (n,1) and (1,1) gives slope 0 (horizontal) which is non-sunny. (n,1) and (1,2) gives slope (2-1)/(1-n) = 1/(1-n) = -1/(n-1). That's not 0, not infinite, not -1 (unless n=2, but n≥3). So slope = -1/(n-1) which is not -1 (except n=2). So that line is sunny (since slope is not 0, infinite, or -1). So we can use a sunny line through (n,1) and (1,2). That line is not parallel to x-axis (slope not 0), not vertical (slope finite), not slope -1 (slope -1/(n-1) ≠ -1). So it's sunny. So we can have n-1 vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1, and a sunny line through (n,1) and (1,2). That covers all