olumn n-2 forces a vertical line. So we have at least one non-sunny line (vertical). So we have n-1 other lines, of which exactly k are sunny. So we have at most k sunny lines.\n\nNow the solution says analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\n\nLet's examine column x=n-1: Points (n-1,b) with b ≤ n+1-(n-1) = 2. So points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column x=n: points (n,1) only.\n\nWe have a vertical line at x=n-2 covering (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Now we need to cover points in columns n-1 and n. We have n-3 remaining columns (x=1,...,x=n-3) each with at least 2 points? Actually column x=1 has points (1,b) with b ≤ n. So many points. We can cover those with vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). That would be n-2 vertical lines total (including x=n-2). But we only have n lines total, and we need exactly k sunny lines. If we use vertical lines for all columns except maybe some, we would have many non-sunny lines. But we can also use sunny lines to cover some columns.\n\nBut the solution's argument is that with only 2 sunny lines, we cannot cover column n-1 and n. Let's examine possibilities.\n\nWe have n lines total, exactly 2 sunny lines. So we have n-2 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. We already have at least one vertical line at x=n-2. We can also use vertical lines for other columns. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, which is enough to have vertical lines for all columns except maybe some. Since there are n columns (x=1,...,x=n). If we use vertical lines for all columns, we need n vertical lines, which would be n non-sunny lines, but we have only n-2 non-sunny lines (since we need 2 sunny lines). So we cannot have vertical lines for all columns; we need to use sunny lines to cover some columns.\n\nWe need to cover all points. 